# Copy to .env, or run `make env`, which does it for you with a fresh random value.
# Every value below is a placeholder. None of them is, or ever was, a real credential.

# --- the ledger ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hex, at least 32 characters. Keys the HMAC that links every ledger record to the one before it. Whoever holds it can
# rebuild history, so it is used for nothing else, and a receipt kept OUTSIDE the data directory is what exposes a
# rewrite. A value that is not hex makes the server refuse to start, on purpose.
# If you delete .env and generate a new key, delete the data volume too (make reset): the old ledger was signed
# with the old key.
LEASH_LEDGER_KEY=replace-with-64-random-hex-characters

# --- optional --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEASH_PORT=8090
#
# A real local model. Without it two deterministic stand-ins answer, so every defence can be exercised anywhere.
# The gateway accepts only a loopback address here, so this works when you run the binary on your own machine
# (cargo run -p leash-server), not from inside the container, whose loopback is its own.
# LEASH_OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# LEASH_OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:1b
#
# Extra host names the server answers to (comma-separated, no ports). Everything else is refused, which is what
# defeats DNS rebinding. The loopback names are always allowed.
# LEASH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
